# Linux Fundamentals


Linux is a free and open-source operating system built around the Linux kernel, 
the core component responsible for managing hardware resources and enabling communication
between software and hardware. It is known for its stability, security, flexibility, and 
performance, making it one of the most widely used operating systems in servers, cloud computing, 
embedded systems, and cybersecurity.

## Kernel vs Distro

It is important to distinguish between the Linux kernel and a Linux distribution (distro). The
kernel is the core of the operating system that manages processes, memory, hardware, and system
resources. A distribution combines the Linux kernel with system utilities, software packages, and
a user interface to create a complete operating system. Popular Linux distributions include Ubuntu,
Debian, Fedora, Arch Linux, CentOS, and Kali Linux, each designed for different purposes.

## Everything is a file

Linux is built on the philosophy that "everything is a file." This means that files, directories,
hardware devices, and many system resources are accessed through a unified file system. The Linux file
system begins at the root directory (/), from which all other directories branch. Important directories
include:

/home – User home directories and personal files.
/etc – System configuration files.
/bin – Essential user commands and executable programs.
/usr – User applications and shared system files.
/var – Variable data such as logs and databases.
/tmp – Temporary files.
/proc – Virtual files containing process and kernel information.
/dev – Device files representing hardware components.

## Users, Groups and Permissions

Linux also uses users, groups, and permissions to control access to system resources.
Every file and directory has an owner, an associated group, and a set of permissions that
determine who can read (r), write (w), or execute (x) it. The root user has unrestricted access
to the system, while regular users are granted only the permissions they need. This follows the
Principle of Least Privilege, which minimizes security risks by limiting unnecessary access.

## Significance

Linux is a cornerstone of cybersecurity because it powers most web servers, cloud platforms,
and network infrastructure. Security professionals rely on Linux for penetration testing,
digital forensics, malware analysis, incident response, and system administration. A solid
understanding of Linux fundamentals provides the foundation for learning advanced cybersecurity
concepts and using industry-standard security tools.